      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "The given shapes are $\\triangle ABC$ and $\\triangle ABD$. \nTo solve this problem, we will utilize the Pythagorean Theorem.\nThe Pythagorean Theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the two other sides.\nFor $\\triangle ABC$, let $h$ be the length of the hypotenuse and $P$ and $Q$ be the lengths of the other two sides.\nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem, we have:\n\\[h^2 = P^2 + Q^2\\]\nThe Pythagorean Theorem can also be applied to $\\triangle ABD$. Let $h_1$ be the length of the hypotenuse and $P_1$ and $Q_1$ be the lengths of the other two sides. \nUsing the Pythagorean Theorem again, we have:\n\\[h_1^2 = P_1^2 + Q_1^2\\]\nGiven below is an image showing $\\triangle ABC$.\n[asy]\npair A, B, C, D, X, Y, Z;\nA=(0,0);\nB=(0,12);\nC=(-16,0);\nD=(-35,0);\nY=(B+D)/2;\nZ=(D+C)/2;\nlabel(\"A\", A, SE);\nlabel(\"B\", B, NE);\nlabel(\"D\", D, SW);\ndraw(A--B--D--A);\ndraw(B--C);\ndraw((0,1.5)--(-1.5,1.5)--(-1.5,0));\nlabel(\"$37$\", (B+D)/2, NW);\nlabel(\"$19$\", (C+D)/2, S);\nlabel(\"$16$\", (A+C)/2, S);\nlabel(\"C\", C, S);\n[/asy]\nFirst, we identify that $P=P_1$ and $Q=16$ since $AC^2 = CQ^2$.\nSecond, we identify that $P=37$ and $Q=31$ since $BC^2 = BQ^2$.\nTherefore, $h^2=948$ and $h=i^{2}i^{2}=184$.\nWe can also identify that $h_1^2=1156=37^2$ and $h_1=i^2i^2=184$.\nThus, $BC=d^{2}-m^{2}=h^{2}-h_1^2=948-1156=296$.\nWe conclude with a boxed final answer of $\\boxed{296}$. The final answer is: 296",
